  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract activity in Louisiana for FY2026 to date totals $5.6B in obligations across 23,788 awards, with an average award size of $235K and obligations up 17.6% year over year. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $2.7B, supported by large prime awards to named shipbuilders. The Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Defense are the two biggest buying agencies, with $2.5B and $2B in obligations respectively. BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. is the top vendor with $2.3B, followed by STRATEGIC STORAGE PARTNERS, LLC. and PITTMAN-SEALEVEL, L.L.C. at $430M and $420M. Other notable industry categories include Facilities Support Services at $561M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $534M. This snapshot covers partial fiscal-year totals and reflects where federal dollars have concentrated in the state so far this fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove FY2026 federal obligations in Louisiana?",
        "body": "Ship Building and Repairing drove a large share of obligations, totaling $2.7B. Major shipbuilding vendors, led by BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. at $2.3B, captured much of that industry spend."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are obligations across agencies and vendors?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Defense together account for the bulk of buying, at $2.5B and $2B respectively. A small set of vendors and industries account for a large portion of the total obligations, as shown by the top vendors and top industries lists."
      }
    ]
  },
